  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nEarly morning RAP analysis and water vapor imagery shows a shortwave \ntrough lifting through the eastern Dakotas, with an associated \nsurface low located over NW Iowa. A weak warm advection regime and \nisentropic ascent ahead of these features is resulting in light rain \nshowers spreading into the western and southcentral UP this morning, \nwith temperatures mainly in the 30s across the area. Chances for \nlight rain will persist through the day today, mainly across the \nwestern half of Upper Michigan as the surface low tracks northeast \nacross Minnesota and grazes the western tip of Lake Superior this \nevening. Expect only minimal precipitation amounts of a tenth of an \ninch or less for most of the area today as the highest QPF values \nremain west of the UP, though a few spots in the west from the \nPorcupine Mountains to the Keweenaw could see slightly higher \namounts approaching a quarter inch (30% chance per latest HREF). \nBreezy south-southeast winds will also develop today as the pressure \ngradient tightens between the approaching low and surface high \npressure over northern Ontario, yielding widespread gusts to 20 mph \nacross the UP and gusts as high as 30 mph for favored downslope \nareas near Lake Superior. Temperatures will be a bit warmer than \nyesterday across the area, with highs in the 50s for most locations.\n\nTemperatures will spike well above normal on Wednesday as continued \nwarm advection sends 850 mb temps to 10-12 C and allows surface \ntemps to reach the upper 60s and perhaps even 70 degrees in a few \nlocations Wednesday afternoon. However, the warm up will be short \nlived as another low forming in the lee of the Colorado Rockies \ntracks to northern Ontario and drags a cold front across the UP \nWednesday night. This will bring another round of rain Wednesday \nevening through Thursday morning. Rainfall amounts do not look \nparticularly impressive, but the NBM does depict a 50-75% chance for \na wetting rain of a quarter inch over roughly the western third of \nthe UP by Thursday morning. Those probabilities quickly drop off to \n15-25% across the central and eastern UP. Modest instability on the \norder of a few hundred J/kg MUCAPE could also produce a few rumbles \nof thunder as the front passes through the area. Once precipitation \nclears the eastern UP Thursday morning, dry weather looks \nincreasingly likely for the rest of THursday through Friday as high \npressure builds back into the region from the northern Plains while \ntemperatures moderate back down into the 50s for the end of the work \nweek. Medium range guidance then hints at another system approaching \nthe region with additional rain chances this weekend, but with a \nrelatively high degree of spread at this time.",
